    gist: 'Greene’s Beauty has been looked at since early childhood. Rooms change when they walk in; people turn generous, nervous, eager to please. It sounds like luck, and in many ways it is, but the book points to its cost. A person admired for their surface starts to suspect that the surface is all anyone wants, and the suspicion grows as the years begin to threaten it. So the Beauty, so often pursued, can be strangely hard to reach. Compliments on their looks bounce off: they have heard them all. The way in, the book suggests, is to value what others overlook, their mind, talents and character, and then to step back now and then so that, for once, they are the one who pursues.',
