    gist: 'Some people never quite agree to grow up. Greene’s Aging Baby is charming in their twenties, intriguing in their thirties and, the book warns, a little wearing by their forties. They keep the best of childhood, its play, enthusiasm and appetite for fun, and quietly hand the rest of life to others. They are often warm, funny and generous with their attention. What they seek in love is not an equal but a parent: someone calm and responsible who will take care of the practical world. The book’s advice is to play that part without ever criticising or judging, and an intense, almost filial attachment forms. Underneath lies an old wish to be looked after without conditions.',
